WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.689
Last time we said that we can’t predict
exactly what will happen when we measure a

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:09.540
quantum system. But the Born rules tells us
how likely the possible out comes are.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.340
In this video I’ll first teach you the easy
version of this rule that works for most simple

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.991
cases and will work for the all the stuff
we’ll do in the main quantum videos. Then

00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:23.340
if you feel like it you can stick around for
the proper rule as well.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:29.110
So as usual, we have our wavefunction in a
superposition of all the possibilities. If

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.800
I measure, it will collapse to just one of
these. To find the probability that it will

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.839
collapse to this state, all I have to do is
take that coefficient...and square it. That’s

00:00:40.839 --> 00:00:47.609
all. If I had a superposition like this: then
the probability the cat is alive is half,

00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:50.510
and dead is also half.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:56.719
This version of the Born rule is very easy.
The actual rule is only slightly more complex,

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:01.389
in that it’s a little bit more involved,
but it isn’t hard at all. I’ll explain

00:01:01.389 --> 00:01:05.700
that now if you’re interested- you won’t
need it for my main quantum videos, but you

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.810
will need it for any of the optional videos
I make on the maths of quantum mechanics.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:15.210
So it’s up to you, skip to the next video
if you like.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:19.740
Previously we looked at the case where these
coefficients are real numbers- the kind of

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.180
numbers we’re all used to using. But actually,
in quantum mechanics we are allowed to use

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.649
a more general type of number called complex
numbers.

00:01:28.649 --> 00:01:33.619
I think the most useful way to think of complex
numbers for quantum mechanics is to see them

00:01:33.619 --> 00:01:35.340
as arrows.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:41.820
First, real numbers are kind of like arrows
too, pointing this or that way if they’re

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.890
negative. Their length is just the absolute
value of the number. Complex numbers generalise

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:52.609
real numbers, because they are arrows that
are allowed to point in any direction.

00:01:52.609 --> 00:01:57.509
So the coefficients in the wavefunction are
like little arrows of different lengths. If

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:01.950
they’re all real, the arrows point left
or right. If they’re complex, then they

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:08.149
can point any direction. But the Born Rule
is the same in both cases: if you want the

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:15.010
probability of this state, look at the arrow
in front of it and find it’s length squared.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.610
In real numbers, this is exactly the same
as squaring the number.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:25.050
Let’s look at this example. If you have
this coefficient in front of this state, it’s

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:33.320
length is root 3 on 4, so it’s probability
is 3/4. But what if I rotated this arrow so

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:39.821
it’s not real anymore? The length is the
same, so the probability stays the same. You

00:02:39.821 --> 00:02:45.300
might be wondering then, what’s the point
of using the complex numbers. It doesn’t

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.840
actually matter at all to the Born Rule, but
it really matters for what we’re looking

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:57.610
at in the next couple of videos, and so those
two states are actually very different physically.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:02.440
So that’s the Born rule. But drawing little
arrows with the exact right length and angle

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.350
is really tedious, so I’ll incase you haven’t
seen it before, I’ll introduce you to the

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:12.890
more standard notation for complex numbers.
If we have an arrow like this, then we call

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:19.080
this direction the real direction, and that
direction the imaginary one. Then we want

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.420
to know how far we need to go in each way
to get to the end of an arrow. In this case

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:30.480
it’s d units in the real direction, e units
in the imaginary one, which you then write

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:37.840
as d+e i, where i represents one unit in the
imaginary direction. So instead of drawing

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:46.290
the arrow, we can just write down this. Here
are some other examples. This is really useful,

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:53.930
because now we can write wavefunctions more
compactly, for example:

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.000
And now it’s your favourite time of the
video! Homework time

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.420
By the way, I’ll type the questions up in
the description as well.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:09.370
First a math one. If I have a complex number
a+bi, use pythagoros’ rule to find the length

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:14.930
of it. So what the probability of a state
with this coefficient?

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:19.780
Then of course, an interpretations question.
Many people have found the use of complex

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:26.150
numbers in the wavefunction very significant,
saying this shows that the wavefunction can’t

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:32.440
actually be real then. In other words, if
can’t be a real physical object. If so,

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:38.190
what kinds of interpretations of the wavefunction
would it rule out? But also, what do you think

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:43.220
of this conclusion, that something described
by complex numbers couldn’t actually represent

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:53.250
‘physical reality’? Are people taking
the word imaginary too literally?

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.370
This probably wasn’t the easiest video you
could have been watching on Youtube today,

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:59.280
so thanks for putting in the effort.

